WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.980
Hi folks, how are you? All good?

2
00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:07.700
In this video we'll debunk the myth that cooking food for the whole week takes a lot of time,

3
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:10.220
that it's inconvenient, expensive and so on.

4
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.460
Now we'll go to the market, get meat, vegetables and a few other things,

5
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:20.060
and then we'll go to my place, cook all of this and explain a bit about how to get well organized.

6
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.760
So that during the week cooking takes you minimal time,

7
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.120
and that at the same time it's healthy, tasty, easy to cook, and everything else.

8
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.860
Let's go to Bajloni, bro, see what vegetables there are,

9
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:36.420
the market is greener and more colorful now than ever, so you don't know what to grab.

10
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.540
Going for good meat — why so?

11
00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:10.580
Because it's worth the effort, bro: meat is a building material, it becomes part of you.

12
00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:15.860
And if you buy not good meat but any old meat, then you'll be any old person yourself and won't even know it.

13
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.820
You see what's happening: nobody wants to be just anybody anymore, look at the line for meat, listen.

14
00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.000
Well, it's good meat, after all.

15
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:38.450
So, we have everything we need: a kilo of turkey and a kilo of rump steak — that's the main thing.

16
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:43.190
Besides that, I bought a few more good quick options that we won't reveal today, or maybe we will.

17
00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:49.050
We'll see; anyway the next scene is in the kitchen, where everything gets cooked.

18
00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:52.930
And here we are in the kitchen, folks, haven't seen each other here for a long time, have we?

19
00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:57.430
I missed it too, but honestly, this summer I cooked a bit less.

20
00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:01.730
We've just laid everything out here, and now a short talk about all this.

21
00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:07.170
So, this isn't exactly a video about how to cook all the food at once and eat only that for a whole week.

22
00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:11.050
I don't really understand this American way where you have everything sort of ready,

23
00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.730
and you just take it out and shove it in the microwave and all that.

24
00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:18.030
Again, you can do it that way with these ingredients, I'll give you options for every case.

25
00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:21.330
But I think this is a good weekend prep,

26
00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:25.850
so that during the week at breakfast you can make all the food for the day.

27
00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:31.290
Plus not everything has to be fried — you'll see how, and again you'll find your own options.

28
00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:34.590
So, what do we have here: first we'll cook the carbs.

29
00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:40.250
We have a kilo of sweet potatoes, a kilo of batata, I'll put it on to boil, as well as white rice.

30
00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:45.370
Then meat: we have that piece of rump steak we bought at "Morava," you saw that.

31
00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:47.310
It's a regular piece of rump steak like this.

32
00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:53.630
Make sure, of course, that it's raised with as few antibiotics as possible, so you know the animal could roam.

33
00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:58.710
And, among other things, we have a kilo of white turkey meat, already sliced into fillets.

34
00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.930
And there are some vegetables, and of course eggs.

35
00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:07.170
We'll make a frittata right away and give a couple of breakfast options, to keep them as simple as possible.

36
00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.050
And we have a mountain of vegetables, peppers.

37
00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.430
"Ox heart," my bro.

38
00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:17.750
"Ox heart" tomatoes are still at home — their season is passing, but tomatoes will still be around for a while.

39
00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:21.130
Beets, yes, beets — it's store-bought.

40
00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.550
This whole box costs a hundred dinars.

41
00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.590
I didn't want to get into boiling beets now, so that it wouldn't go to waste.

42
00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:31.730
But anyone who wants to save time can just get beets for a hundred dinars.

43
00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:36.250
Or can buy for 50 dinars, for 30 dinars a kilo, and boil the beets themselves.

44
00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:41.110
There's zucchini, there's cabbage — you'll see now.

45
00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:43.530
To start, we'll boil the carbs.

46
00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:52.810
Bro, this sweet potato for 440 dinars looks decent to me, unlike the one at "Maxi," honestly.

47
00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:54.170
How much is the batata, 440?

48
00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:59.090
Yes, we didn't want to go into "Maxi," we bought the batata at the market.

49
00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:04.410
I warned Martina it would hurt: she said 440 dinars a kilo.

50
00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.710
You understand, that's quite a sum.

51
00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:11.430
But you can, bro, take potatoes, or something else.

52
00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:16.170
I like batata, and batata can sit for several days — that's how I'll prepare it.

53
00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:18.650
That is, just olive oil — and let it sit in the fridge.

54
00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:43.760
The water is heating; anyone who didn't realize that you can bring water to a boil before cooking potatoes

55
00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:45.520
lives in the nineteenth century.

56
00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.260
The next thing we need to do is cook rice.

57
00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.860
What you need to know about rice: you need to try cooking it once to understand how it cooks.

58
00:04:54.220 --> 00:05:00.800
Don't ask me how much water to add or how much rice — it's done by eye.

59
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:07.140
You pour plenty of water, then taste, and when the rice is properly cooked, when it can be eaten,

60
00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:09.800
you strain it — and there you have cooked rice.

61
00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:16.020
That is, there's no special recipe for rice — you just pour boiling water over it and cook.

62
00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:22.880
Now on to breakfast: so that it's ready when you need it and when you have no time, when you're in a rush,

63
00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.620
you need to somehow prepare the eggs so they're good tomorrow too.

64
00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:28.040
If you make scrambled eggs to wait for you until tomorrow,

65
00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:32.120
that's probably one of the worst experiences on your fitness journey.

66
00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:36.280
But if you make a frittata — it bakes in the oven, can sit in the fridge,

67
00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:40.880
you can eat it cold, you can eat it warm, you can reheat it, and that's great.

68
00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:45.240
I'll show you how it's done now, and if you don't have time for that, if you didn't manage,

69
00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:49.480
eggs can be boiled anytime and combined with something from this.

70
00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:51.720
So we'll slowly start.

71
00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.080
And most importantly — we'll eat deliciously,

72
00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.000
we won't allow ourselves to make preps that will be tasteless.

73
00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.080
Like "I eat it by force, but at least I'm full" — that's not my approach.

74
00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:07.160
A frittata is basically baked eggs with some vegetables, you can add sweet potato too,

75
00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:10.360
you can add anything, potatoes would literally go in there too.

76
00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:13.340
Only depending on what you want to cook,

77
00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:17.160
search the internet a bit, ask around, see what needs processing beforehand.

78
00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:23.140
I'll take a pan, just lightly fry this pepper — no spinach — and zucchini.

79
00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.660
And then pour the eggs over it and put it in the oven for about half an hour.

80
00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.520
And we'll see what comes of it.

81
00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:47.890
I've cracked the eggs, they're basically ready, I'll salt and pepper them right away.

82
00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:54.110
What else I like to add to eggs: they get even yellower if you add turmeric,

83
00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:57.190
it's a good trick and a very good antioxidant.

84
00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:03.310
So I highly recommend it, you can buy organic everywhere: at "Idea," in health food stores — they have it.

85
00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:07.930
I seriously love caraway as a seasoning, and you can make it slightly spicy too.

86
00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:11.530
I cracked eight eggs — that's enough for today and tomorrow.

87
00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:18.510
I think that's a good ratio, and then you either eat boiled eggs or make another one.

88
00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:20.990
But of course, someone who needs to save a lot of time

89
00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:25.970
can eat for five, even seven days what they prepared on the first day as a frittata.

90
00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:30.110
Anyway, a frittata behaves great in the fridge. There, the pan is hot.

91
00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:37.090
I'll take olive oil, which I think gives a better flavor when it comes to vegetables,

92
00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:38.570
but it's more or less the same.

93
00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:43.350
So, you need to fry the vegetables, transfer them here, pour the eggs over the top —

94
00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:48.010
and it goes into the oven for half an hour, and while the extractor fan is running, you can't hear us.

95
00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:06.870
Like that, bro. I won't process these vegetables too much, because otherwise they'll be too dry once baked in the oven.

96
00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:12.350
I think you only need to soften them a bit so they're not completely raw — that's the point of a frittata.

97
00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:18.290
With the spinach I didn't want to bother much at all, I'll put the spinach right here into the eggs,

98
00:08:18.290 --> 00:08:20.570
because then it will blend in properly with them.

99
00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:27.190
There, that's basically it. Then all of this goes into the oven together for half an hour, maybe forty minutes,

100
00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:31.570
but what you need to know here: a frittata is like a pie, like gibanica.

101
00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:35.990
You literally just poke it, and if you see no runny egg left on it,

102
00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:38.430
it's done — and that's it, there's nothing more to it.

103
00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:55.500
The next thing that happens: the batata is ready, it needs five minutes to cook, no more.

104
00:08:55.500 --> 00:09:01.300
So all is well, I'll set it to drain, and as for it, it's more or less ready,

105
00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:03.740
I pour olive oil on it, and from Lastovo at that.

106
00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:09.060
Needless to say, cold-pressed: there's no such industry there, this is oil from brother Ivan.

107
00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:13.080
So this batata is ready, and what I like about it —

108
00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:18.300
it stays really good in the fridge for about three days, so people eat it straight from there.

109
00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.880
I'll just stir a little so the olive oil spreads — and that's it.

110
00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:28.520
Our next task is this rump steak, it's the heart of it all, the main thing in this video.

111
00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:32.940
All you need to do is leave it for a while at room temperature so it's soft.

112
00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:38.040
It will be soft now, and when cooked, if it has rested at room temperature,

113
00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:42.260
and don't be afraid to leave it for five hours at room temperature too.

114
00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:45.440
It won't spoil from that, it'll only get better.

115
00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:52.260
I only need to sear it over high heat on all sides, that's my only task now,

116
00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:55.340
and then it goes into the oven to finish for about fifteen minutes.

117
00:09:55.340 --> 00:10:01.000
That's how you get rump steak; you can, of course, do beefsteak too, which is good to eat all week,

118
00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:06.500
you can even cook veal this way, say a veal leg, but that's for another time, now let's deal with this.

119
00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:10.720
I'm just trying to tell you everything because now the "flying saucer" will switch on again.

120
00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:47.670
The meat has seared on all sides, and what's very good about this —

121
00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:53.030
the meat is seared on the burner, fully "sealed," and then finishes cooking inside in the oven.

122
00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:57.550
And I'll help it a little with these three cloves of garlic, let them give up some flavor,

123
00:10:57.550 --> 00:11:04.110
I like to eat it cooked when it's not during the day, when there are no vampires to be driven away.

124
00:11:10.510 --> 00:11:15.410
Oops, the frittata is about to be done, maybe it's done already, but let it stand a bit longer,

125
00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:19.310
and we'll deal with the vegetables now, and that will be more or less everything.

126
00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:25.610
The carbs are ready, the rice is here, I put it literally like this, covered, in the fridge,

127
00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:30.130
and then I just take it out during the week, and really for about four days it's fine.

128
00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:37.330
The batata the same, three or four days, I just cover it with a lid, add olive oil and salt — and that's it,

129
00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:38.530
they're literally ready.

130
00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:42.750
That is, at any moment you can just take it, transfer it to another container and take it with you.

131
00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:47.730
One more thing, I recommend it all the time, always when we talk about organization, about prep:

132
00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:50.670
grate carrots, cabbage, you can do beets.

133
00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:55.930
The beet is like this now, so fine, I won't grate it, but this I'll prepare very quickly.

134
00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:08.440
At some point, when you learn to cook like this,

135
00:12:08.440 --> 00:12:12.360
you can, if you don't live alone, hand off kitchen cleanup to someone else.

136
00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:15.120
That's exactly what I'm thinking about: I grate, everything flies in all directions,

137
00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:21.780
but basically if you clean yourself, it's better to get right to work — that's what I was doing just now.

138
00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:28.300
I'm literally cooking and cleaning all the time, so basically, when everything is ready, the kitchen will be clean.

139
00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:31.580
For me this is a good cook — an operative.

140
00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:39.480
One small addition — it's not mandatory, of course.

141
00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:43.340
You don't have to do this, but you can.

142
00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:49.900
I'll just toss in this garlic, let it fry a little, and I'll brush the meat with mustard.

143
00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:54.440
It comes out very tasty, I just didn't do it at the beginning so that the mustard wouldn't burn,

144
00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:59.900
and this way the mustard stays "alive," just warm, a crust sets slightly — and it'll be fire.

145
00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:07.980
While all this is baking, I took one last thing — turkey fillet,

146
00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:11.060
of course, it could be chicken, it could be anything else.

147
00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:15.960
I also bought Angus burgers, so here it's basically up to you.

148
00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:19.500
And this thing I wouldn't fry in advance.

149
00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:24.840
Though I think it's good, perhaps, to fry it in the morning for the second half of the day, if there's no other option.

150
00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:30.820
But what I think you should do in any case: set aside about fifteen minutes each morning,

151
00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:33.820
you cook yourself some oatmeal, which you'll eat with eggs,

152
00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:39.580
fry meat for one meal, the second from the oven, right, this rump steak, it's already baked.

153
00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:48.500
And basically that way we've made life easier for ourselves and reduced all the cooking for a whole day to, say, ten to fifteen minutes.

154
00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:54.600
We'll sum up soon, I'll just salt, pepper and pour olive oil over it,

155
00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:58.600
so it marinates, and then literally two or three minutes on the burner — and that's it.

156
00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:13.640
Oh, Martin, I told you this frittata would be a bomb, look at it, listen.

157
00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:17.140
Wait, I'm going to do something to it now.

158
00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:21.800
Oh, yes, this is just it.

159
00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:24.080
It couldn't be better.

160
00:14:27.090 --> 00:14:30.330
Oh, ready, here it is.

161
00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:35.910
Rump steak is best when it's cooled, but it's good when it hasn't cooled too.

162
00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:41.870
Anyway, now it has to be left for fifteen to twenty minutes to finish cooking through inside.

163
00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:45.630
But it looks insanely cool, bro.

164
00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:46.550
Well, folks.

165
00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:51.390
Let's sum up everything we did in the kitchen — what I did while you watched.

166
00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:58.450
So, first I took and cooked the rice and sweet potatoes, because they're the easiest to cook: boiling water — and done.

167
00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:04.950
Then I started making the frittata: lightly sautéed the vegetables, poured the eggs over and left it for half an hour in the oven.

168
00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:10.350
Then I took the rump steak, simply seared it on all sides and put it in the oven for fifteen minutes.

169
00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:12.110
Brushed it with mustard — that's optional.

170
00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:17.450
And then I dealt with the vegetables — grated these carrots and cabbage.

171
00:15:18.070 --> 00:15:24.090
The beets I "cooked" like this: I paid a hundred dinars for them at the market instead of thirty — what can you do.

172
00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:28.210
There are also fresh vegetables here, and I marinated the turkey fillet,

173
00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:31.390
so it can be thrown into the pan at any moment.

174
00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:36.250
But again, you can fry it in advance too, and a very good option is the oven.

175
00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:39.090
That is, you can fry it in a pan, everyone does it that way, of course,

176
00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:43.670
but if you do it in the oven, you put baking paper and just lay them out in little piles.

177
00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:48.250
You can wrap them in something so they stay more or less juicy — that's good.

178
00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:53.070
Now I'll just tidy this up somehow, and we'll go to the table and discuss all this.

179
00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:58.090
Well, folks, everything's ready, and the prep for next week is done.

180
00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:01.910
And first of all, I should probably show you what it looks like.

181
00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:05.470
And I myself, honestly, am curious how the frittata turned out.

182
00:16:06.650 --> 00:16:07.210
Oh...

183
00:16:08.230 --> 00:16:10.790
Here, for Ricky Martin, my bro.

184
00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:16.730
You know what's cool? When you film videos like this where you cook food, at the end you also have to eat it.

185
00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:20.690
And we try not to throw anything away. We immediately remember those kids in Africa and all that.

186
00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:22.790
Anyway, the frittata is great.

187
00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:27.690
What you need to do: take it out of what it was cooked in, so it doesn't steam.

188
00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:29.390
That is, opening it up like this is also fine.

189
00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:32.010
And then, of course, put it in the fridge as usual.

190
00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:33.330
That was the first thing.

191
00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:39.730
The second thing, which interests all of us more, of course, is how the rump steak turned out.

192
00:16:42.970 --> 00:16:45.510
Oh, Martin, huh?

193
00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:49.130
Great stuff.

194
00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:52.690
It means that inside it's slightly rare, as it should be.

195
00:16:52.810 --> 00:16:57.110
That is, once it dries a bit, and when it cools, the blood won't run.

196
00:16:57.110 --> 00:16:59.650
But it's a great thing.

197
00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:02.350
Well, and now I'll sum all this up a bit.

198
00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:06.550
So, with this kind of prep you can save a lot of time during the week

199
00:17:06.550 --> 00:17:10.810
and still eat quality food that you bought and cooked yourself.

200
00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:13.370
You know what ingredients you use, and so on.

201
00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:16.450
I love this, I adore it, like any food delivery,

202
00:17:16.450 --> 00:17:20.470
but I think this way of cooking has a huge advantage.

203
00:17:20.610 --> 00:17:23.070
Mostly because you're not chasing savings on food.

204
00:17:23.070 --> 00:17:28.730
You'd save too little per month or per year if you bought cheaper ingredients.

205
00:17:28.950 --> 00:17:33.790
Whether it's frying oil, seasonings or the products themselves of worse quality.

206
00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:39.230
The scheme is this: you make yourself a frittata, that is, for breakfasts,

207
00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:43.150
and you can make it for two, three, even five days, just let it sit.

208
00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:44.650
You only need good containers.

209
00:17:44.950 --> 00:17:49.770
Otherwise, if you absolutely have to prep for five days, boil yourself some eggs.

210
00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:52.830
Here, for example, there are eight eggs in this frittata,

211
00:17:52.830 --> 00:17:56.810
which means I could boil another twelve eggs, and that would be enough for all the workdays.

212
00:17:57.010 --> 00:17:58.110
But, bro, that's not what I'm after.

213
00:17:58.230 --> 00:18:01.810
Really: you set aside time in the morning, get up a bit earlier — and that's it.

214
00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:12.370
As for breakfast, there are enough vegetables here, there are these options, we'll talk about them further now.

215
00:18:12.370 --> 00:18:16.470
As for lunch, here we have a kilo of rump steak,

216
00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:22.130
and I figured that every day you have two hundred grams of rump steak for lunch, for the main meal.

217
00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:25.910
Red meat: it has creatine, it has iron — great.

218
00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:32.970
And as for evening meat dishes, here I took Angus pljeskavice.

219
00:18:33.090 --> 00:18:37.550
You just have to see what the option is, and of course you can make it yourself:

220
00:18:37.550 --> 00:18:42.990
you grind half a kilo of beef, add salt, pepper, make portions, and when it's time to cook,

221
00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:47.370
add one egg, knead and shape the pljeskavice — also a fine option, right?

222
00:18:47.550 --> 00:18:51.250
And what's even more important here: I marinated this turkey.

223
00:18:51.250 --> 00:18:56.210
I'll pour in more olive oil, right now it's done this way to make it easier to handle,

224
00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:58.810
but really it should be floating in this oil,

225
00:18:59.190 --> 00:19:03.490
so you take something deep enough, pack it down so it's even, and pour oil over it.

226
00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:07.510
And here we have fairly simple things: this rice, just boiled,

227
00:19:07.510 --> 00:19:09.870
you only need to cover it when it cools,

228
00:19:10.070 --> 00:19:16.790
with it, say, three or four days is fine, even five, the batata the same — it will keep four or five days,

229
00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:22.210
I put salt, pepper, olive oil on it, and, of course, besides eating it like this, cold,

230
00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:25.230
you can heat it, you can add it to dishes — and it's great.

231
00:19:25.770 --> 00:19:31.270
Of the vegetables the prep — you saw it yourselves — of cabbage and carrots looks like this,

232
00:19:31.430 --> 00:19:35.330
you can add cabbage of another color here, you can add beets,

233
00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:41.790
but I put in the beets so as not to duplicate; it costs eighty to a hundred dinars at the market,

234
00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:49.110
and I think that's fair, considering that someone buys, boils, peels and cuts the beets — I think it's perfectly fine.

235
00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:53.170
I deliberately put an "ox heart" tomato here — I'll mention it once more,

236
00:19:53.510 --> 00:19:59.890
this unassuming creature is actually very nutritious and very tasty, sweet,

237
00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:05.470
and of course we have green salad, there's still plenty of it, there are those store-bought options from "Maxi,"

238
00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:11.450
and of course there's also a mountain of fresh vegetables, a mountain of everything you can sauté quickly,

239
00:20:11.710 --> 00:20:16.030
but the point of all this is to set aside an hour or two on the weekend,

240
00:20:16.190 --> 00:20:19.390
get well organized and make the whole week easier for yourself.

241
00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:21.950
This meat, I think, is an excellent option,

242
00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:26.190
because it's one of the rare ways to eat cold meat that stays tasty,

243
00:20:26.190 --> 00:20:30.970
I think this should be cooked all the time: you try this, the rump steak, you try beefsteak,

244
00:20:31.450 --> 00:20:36.790
you even try veal leg — I think it could turn out well, only it needs to be braised longer in the oven,

245
00:20:36.910 --> 00:20:43.290
that's it, this is good organization to start with: it doesn't have to be expensive, doesn't have to take much time,

246
00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:47.630
and to this, of course, you add: you get up in the morning, and you have, for example, these eggs,

247
00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:52.870
you need to cook yourself oatmeal for the day, and during the time you're cooking this oatmeal,

248
00:20:52.870 --> 00:20:57.650
you fry yourself some meat or do something else, but it's fifteen minutes,

249
00:20:57.750 --> 00:20:59.770
you really can't say you're losing this time,

250
00:20:59.770 --> 00:21:04.750
because in any other case, buying from any fast food place and so on,

251
00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:07.990
you'd have to spend ten to fifteen minutes to go, come back and so on,

252
00:21:08.570 --> 00:21:16.090
so there, this saves both time and money, and of course you eat much better quality, much better — that's roughly it.

253
00:21:16.090 --> 00:21:23.470
As for this — you just take it like this, scoop it, plop it, salt, pepper, olive oil — and that's it.

254
00:21:23.710 --> 00:21:28.390
So, folks, that's all in this video, I hope it will help you with organization,

255
00:21:28.570 --> 00:21:34.110
mostly because organization is more than half the job, and thanks to it you'll eat a bit healthier and better.

256
00:21:34.290 --> 00:21:37.170
I'll of course try to throw you more ideas,

257
00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:39.650
and do you know what you can do now?

258
00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:44.130
So you don't miss something else just as cool,

259
00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:47.350
and Martin and I now need to eat this before it gets cold.

260
00:21:48.250 --> 00:21:49.170
See you, bye!